I fixed the PR description: #76 now shows as normal Markdown. I didn't commit anything to this repo, because the bug is in pit, not pi-action.

**What went wrong.** I read the log of the run that opened #76 ([43522489097](https://github.com/cv/pi-action/actions/runs/43522489097)). The agent passed the PR body to its program as a single string: `async ({ … }, body: string) => …`, with the text in `params`. But it sent that text JSON-encoded, wrapped in quotes and with `\n` escapes in place of line breaks. pit only decodes `params` that look like a JSON object or array, and only when the program can't take a string. Here it could take a string, so pit passed the encoded text through unchanged and `gh pr create --body` posted it as is. The agent's next step, which filled in the PR number, read that body back and saved it again without decoding it.

This has only happened once. Of the last 30 PRs, #76 is the only one whose body starts with `"`.

**How to stop it.** The fix goes in `cv/pit`, in `resolveToolInput` (`src/tool/input.ts`). When `params` is a quoted JSON string, pit should decode it, whatever input type the program declares. I made the change against pit v0.36.2 in a local clone, and its `test/tool/input.test.ts` passes (19 tests):

```diff
 export function resolveToolInput(source: string, params: unknown): unknown {
-  if (typeof params !== "string" || programInputAdmitsString(source) !== false) return params;
+  if (typeof params !== "string") return params;
   const text = params.trim();
+  // A string sent JSON-encoded, quotes and \n escapes included, is never meant literally.
+  if (text.length >= 2 && text.startsWith('"') && text.endsWith('"')) {
+    try {
+      const decoded = JSON.parse(text) as unknown;
+      if (typeof decoded === "string") return decoded;
+    } catch {
+      // Not a JSON string: keep it.
+    }
+  }
+  if (programInputAdmitsString(source) !== false) return params;
   if (!(text.startsWith("{") || text.startsWith("["))) return params;
```

The two new tests check that a JSON-encoded string is decoded for a `body: string` program, and that a quoted string that isn't valid JSON (`"a\q"`) is left alone. The catch: a program that really wants a value with literal quotes around it would now get it decoded. That seems unlikely to matter.

**I couldn't push this to `cv/pit`.** The app token has no write access to that repo (it returned a 403), so the patch exists only on this runner, which is deleted when the job ends; the diff above is the only copy. Someone with access will need to open the PR there. When it's released, bump `extensions: github:cv/pit#…` in `.github/workflows/pi-assistant.yml`.

Until then, you could tell the agent to pass long text as an object (`params: { body }`) or to use `gh … --body-file`. That avoids the problem without changing pit.
